expect they're going to be solved in the next few seconds.

60
00:03:59,443 --> 00:04:07,008
And so the deck that we're going to walk through tonight, if either Dr. Cushing or Ms.

61
00:04:07,028 --> 00:04:14,113
Galussi are able to share that, I just got the microphone and camera on one of my computers working.

62
00:04:14,153 --> 00:04:22,219
And so I don't think I'm going to continue to play with this to get to the point where I can also share my screen.

63
00:04:22,240 --> 00:04:23,200
That would seem too ambitious.

64
00:04:23,240 --> 00:04:24,661
I see Dr. Cushing's in the document.

65
00:04:24,701 --> 00:04:26,443
So we should be good to go.

66
00:04:27,903 --> 00:04:37,411
Peter, if I could just do a few more seconds of introduction and then we'll start going through the various slides that we have for you tonight.

67
00:04:37,471 --> 00:04:49,302
So as the superintendent said, tonight is our first formal budget meeting specific to the development of the FY23 budget.

68
00:04:49,883 --> 00:04:52,045
As the school committee is aware,

69
00:04:52,942 --> 00:05:15,508
We have been updating you first quarterly throughout the year, and then over the course of the last month or two, we've been discussing in greater detail both the budgetary priorities for the Medford Public Schools coming into FY23, as well as the budgetary challenges that we know we're going to face coming into this fiscal year.

70
00:05:15,528 --> 00:05:21,289
A lot of them, frankly, are similar to budgetary challenges that we would face in any fiscal year, and others are

71
00:05:22,173 --> 00:05:23,975
very specific to FY23.

72
00:05:24,976 --> 00:05:49,417
As we discussed at your meeting on May 2nd, given that the district is currently in the midst of collective bargaining negotiations with nine of our 10 unions, and given that the vast majority of our operating budget is consumed by personnel costs, as is the case for all school districts,

73
00:05:50,417 --> 00:06:01,141
There is a, that is a significant variable and there's a significant degree of uncertainty that we as an organization have coming into this fiscal year.

74
00:06:02,321 --> 00:06:17,727
That being said, we have a responsibility both to continue to pursue our educational and operational objectives in the upcoming fiscal year

75
00:06:18,303 --> 00:06:41,376
And so we'll talk a little bit tonight about how those two things will interact with one another and how we'll try to adhere to our commitment to the community with respect to transparency and how we'll continue to put student interests and student outcomes at the center of all of our strategic priorities, including those that have a monetary impact.

76
00:06:42,157 --> 00:06:47,060
I will now, as we start to go through the slides, and I think we can go now, Peter, if you're ready,

77
00:06:49,624 --> 00:07:01,852
Whenever we talk about the budget, well, I know for some committee members, and particularly for some of you that have been on the committee for some time, there can be some degree of repetition and redundancy as you hear me walk through the various steps and sequences.

78
00:07:01,912 --> 00:07:09,737
But I think it was Miss McLaughlin who pointed out, as we were sort of mapping out our FY23 budgetary timeline and process,

79
00:07:10,497 --> 00:07:19,740
that the budget really is an opportunity for members of the community to have an opportunity to see how the organization functions, to hear about what priorities we're pursuing.

80
00:07:20,200 --> 00:07:28,783
And so given that, I try to sort of provide a little bit of foundation knowledge at the beginning of the budgetary process, just in terms of how school districts are funded.

81
00:07:29,183 --> 00:07:39,467
I'll go through those as quickly as I can, because again, we have been discussing this on almost a weekly basis over the past month or two, but at the same time, I do think it's important context.

82
00:07:40,654 --> 00:07:49,207
And then in our first substantive slide, you'll see a slightly modified version of a document that you saw last year that walks through the various steps.

83
00:07:49,448 --> 00:07:52,192
And that first step with regard to the strategic plan

84
00:07:52,728 --> 00:07:58,632
is one that I think in any budget process, given how much we have going on, it can be easy to skip over.

85
00:07:58,953 --> 00:08:04,777
But it's important that we start with our goals and what the objectives are that we're looking to pursue.

86
00:08:04,797 --> 00:08:19,327
The members of the school committee, as well as members of the school administration, filled out budget surveys in which essentially you had the opportunity to thinking in the context of the strategic plan, what are the issues and priorities that you believe the district needs to pursue.

87
00:08:20,628 --> 00:08:23,549
at that third step with regard to the budget meetings, that's where we are right now.

88
00:08:23,589 --> 00:08:29,371
This is our opportunity to, in public, flesh out those priorities and to talk a little bit about how they interact with one another.

89
00:08:30,131 --> 00:08:45,977
And really, one of the things that we'll talk about tonight is how we're in a very interesting budgetary time in which we have all the uncertainty and speculation that often comes in Massachusetts with school districts without knowing what the availability of funding is going to be.

90
00:08:46,357 --> 00:08:47,117
And at the same time,

91
00:08:47,587 --> 00:08:57,936
we've been fortunate enough, both on the municipal side and on the school side, through the ARPA and ESSER funding, to have a significant amount of one-time funding resources available.

92
00:08:58,376 --> 00:09:15,290
But some of the strategic considerations that we in the school administration have to take into account, and that you as the school committee have to take into account, is how do we balance those one-time funding priorities against the reoccurring priorities, and how do we determine, essentially, which budget those priorities land on?

93
00:09:15,630 --> 00:09:40,821
And we'll talk a little bit tonight about what the implications of those decisions are, what the risks are for potential deficits that we can't fund in the future, but then also what are the risks for immediate student needs that we know exist right now in the somewhat aftermath, or at least hopefully the later stages of the pandemic and the disruptions that it causes.

94
00:09:41,342 --> 00:09:44,303
These meetings lead up to the superintendent's recommendation

95
00:09:44,739 --> 00:09:46,820
I think oftentimes there's some confusion about that.

96
00:09:47,160 --> 00:09:55,663
There's an assumption that the municipal government sends over a number and the superintendent then puts it in an envelope and hands it over to the school committee and the school committee votes on that.

97
00:09:56,103 --> 00:10:10,648
And I think one of the points that this school committee has made, certainly in the time that I've been in this role and my impression is to some degree prior to that, is that part of the school committee's job and your responsibility is to advocate zealously on behalf of

98
00:10:11,264 --> 00:10:14,706
the school communities that we work with and that we serve.

99
00:10:15,167 --> 00:10:24,913
The municipal government, we know, and this is true for the mayor and it's true for the city council, has that responsibility, but also the responsibility to balance the competing needs of the community.

100
00:10:24,993 --> 00:10:31,778
And we know that we as the school department try to be a good citizen within the constituency of municipal departments.

101
00:10:32,178 --> 00:10:39,783
And so one of the points that I always make at this point in the budget season, and I know you've heard me make it this year as well, is that as the school administration

102
00:10:40,221 --> 00:10:57,078
We understand our responsibility to be planning for all potential contingencies, the information and the priorities that you'll hear about tonight are important priorities based on facts and based on the actual truthful needs of our students.

103
00:10:57,478 --> 00:11:02,143
And at the same time we understand that we are one constituent part of the city of Medford.

104
00:11:02,559 --> 00:11:06,101
and we will be prepared to implement the budget that is appropriated to us.

105
00:11:06,821 --> 00:11:22,828
That is something you've heard me say almost verbatim each time we've talked about this, but this is an important step in that process of us making sure that we're able to articulate fully what are the needs of the school department and through that, the needs of the Medford Public Schools students.

106
00:11:23,208 --> 00:11:25,929
So the superintendent's recommendation will go to the school committee.

107
00:11:25,949 --> 00:11:32,352
The school committee will then make a request based on that recommendation or variation as you see fit to the municipal government.

108
00:11:32,851 --> 00:11:36,294
The municipal government through the mayor and the city council will then make an appropriation.

109
00:11:36,754 --> 00:11:47,683
And then that second vote that you took on June 24th last year for purposes of the FY 22 operating budget is to actually adopt the budget that is appropriated to.

110
00:11:48,123 --> 00:11:51,106
You'll recall that you made a contingent vote last year.

111
00:11:51,146 --> 00:11:53,488
The mayor had made a recommendation to the city council.

112
00:11:54,248 --> 00:11:56,570
It represented a significant increase in our funding.

113
00:11:56,870 --> 00:12:00,513
There was also about, I believe $700,000 less than that was requested.

114
00:12:02,830 --> 00:12:04,692
That was something that we were planning for.

115
00:12:04,852 --> 00:12:14,280
And as you've heard me say before, that the school administration works in conjunction with the city administration to make sure, again, that we're prepared for any potential contingency.

116
00:12:14,840 --> 00:12:21,126
And then after that adoption, after the appropriations made by the municipal council, it is our job to implement that budget.

117
00:12:21,166 --> 00:12:28,332
So you'll see the note there that there are various components of the budget process and different levers of government that have a hand in it.

118
00:12:29,599 --> 00:12:31,680
It's a process that takes place.

119
00:12:32,300 --> 00:12:37,641
There's some variation depending on if it's a regional school committee or things of that nature, a city government versus a town government.

120
00:12:38,121 --> 00:12:45,603
But generally speaking, a process like this plays out in every city and town in Massachusetts throughout, right around this time during the fiscal year.

121
00:12:46,323 --> 00:12:49,644
Okay, that's it for our, sorry, one more slide.

122
00:12:49,704 --> 00:12:50,664
You've seen this one before.

123
00:12:50,684 --> 00:12:56,685
Again, our budget is made up in Medford of about 80% local contribution.

124
00:12:56,705 --> 00:12:59,286
That's money generated by the taxpayers of Medford.

125
00:12:59,862 --> 00:13:18,890
20% or thereabouts, Chapter 70, excuse me, Chapter 70 coming in from the Commonwealth, as we talked about a little bit at your May 2nd meeting, that is based on the belief that every taxpayer in the Commonwealth of Massachusetts has a vested interest in the well-being of the students of Medford.

126
00:13:19,470 --> 00:13:24,592
But the breakdown of the formula is based on the socioeconomic dynamics of the community.

127
00:13:25,153 --> 00:13:27,975
or so says the Massachusetts State Legislature.

128
00:13:28,195 --> 00:13:37,019
And as I said on May 2nd, I would recommend we table for the time being the degree to which we either agree or disagree with the specifics of that formula.

129
00:13:37,720 --> 00:13:42,842
Like many communities with a high degree of need, we would prefer not to have an 80-20 split.

130
00:13:43,663 --> 00:13:48,045
But at the same time, that is what the formula essentially spits out at us right now.

131
00:13:48,445 --> 00:13:50,866
And in this particular fiscal year, in FY23, we're fortunate to, as many

132
00:13:54,371 --> 00:13:58,674
districts have received a significant uptick in our Chapter 70 allocation.

133
00:13:59,534 --> 00:14:12,783
But again, as I reported in our labor update, given the actual breakdown in the ratio of the funding, that has not as consequential an effect as we would have preferred, perhaps, if the numbers were a little different.

134
00:14:13,264 --> 00:14:19,268
You'll see that column on the far right there just representing the external funding that comes into the district.

135
00:14:19,528 --> 00:14:22,730
A large portion of that is in the special education realm.

136
00:14:23,575 --> 00:14:29,597
And we'll talk a little bit tonight about the fluctuation that we have to forecast for year in and year out.

137
00:14:30,577 --> 00:14:32,397
Okay, now I think we can move on.

138
00:14:33,398 --> 00:14:38,939
And again, this is a, I'll move quickly through this slide because it's one that you saw just a week ago.

139
00:14:39,919 --> 00:14:50,962
But in the context of trying to plan for a almost or perhaps over $70 million budget, when you have nine collective bargaining agreements that represent

140
00:14:51,635 --> 00:14:56,359
a very, very large portion of your budgetary expenses on a year-in and year-out basis.

141
00:14:57,220 --> 00:15:03,845
You can't really sort of escape that as an important factor in your budgetary planning.

142
00:15:04,746 --> 00:15:14,434
And that's something we obviously discussed at length last week and something that we'll continue to discuss as we move forward throughout this budget process.

143
00:15:14,855 --> 00:15:17,877
I just want to make quick note of that third bullet point down.

144
00:15:19,151 --> 00:15:26,897
where it says that a priority for the Medford Public Schools is to increase wages to maintain competitiveness while ensuring sustainability.

145
00:15:27,518 --> 00:15:33,983
That is a point that you heard me articulate again in your May 2nd meeting, and I will articulate it again here.

146
00:15:34,643 --> 00:15:42,849
And it is certainly the case that it is a point that we in the administration have articulated in various forums with our various bargaining partners.

147
00:15:43,470 --> 00:15:47,233
We understand that we have a responsibility to maintain competitiveness.

148
00:15:48,040 --> 00:16:01,912
But that responsibility is qualified by the responsibility we have to not establish financial structural deficits that would cause significant problems for the school district and the years ahead.

149
00:16:02,633 --> 00:16:11,340
And so that is our job to find that equilibrium and to negotiate in good faith toward a result that achieves both ends of that objective.

150
00:16:11,998 --> 00:16:20,341
And that's something that will be part of tonight's presentation, but also part of all of our efforts with respect to negotiations moving forward.

151
00:16:21,901 --> 00:16:22,922
If we can go to the next one.

152
00:16:23,422 --> 00:16:26,403
Okay, again, these are the priorities as summarized.

153
00:16:26,443 --> 00:16:37,027
And so as we walk through the specific priorities tonight and some of the initiatives that we'll be recommending that the school district fund through one means or another,

154
00:16:38,780 --> 00:16:43,241
All of them, frankly, should correlate with one or multiple bullet points here.

155
00:16:44,141 --> 00:16:59,806
And so, again, the reason that the negotiations are such an important component of the budgetary process is that we're an organization made up of people, and that's where most of our money goes.

156
00:16:59,886 --> 00:17:03,467
So of our $67 million budget, $67.4 million budget in FY22, 54.3

157
00:17:08,613 --> 00:17:11,954
approximately was money that was put into personnel expenses.

158
00:17:12,034 --> 00:17:27,977
So that means that if we don't know how much the personnel is going to cost us in a given year, it can be very difficult to nail down our specific budgetary priorities that we'll be able to ultimately realize.

159
00:17:28,037 --> 00:17:35,558
But as I said, it's also important for us to continue to articulate those so that the community has a full understanding as to what exactly it is

160
00:17:36,695 --> 00:17:40,277
that we are looking to invest their money in, frankly.

161
00:17:40,817 --> 00:17:49,102
So again, your critical priorities there, and then you'll see them in greater specificity tonight, are making sure that students have the social, emotional, and mental health supports that they need.

162
00:17:50,062 --> 00:17:55,905
To the degree we're able to reduce fee-based services, that's something that this committee has identified as a critical priority.

163
00:17:56,585 --> 00:17:59,967
Making sure that we're going through the responsible expansion of our CTE programming.

164
00:18:00,507 --> 00:18:04,789
That is a point that we'll be discussing in greater detail in tomorrow night's budget meeting.

165
00:18:06,497 --> 00:18:22,462
I'll get into a little bit of the sort of various ways in which we've tried to organize the themes in a moment, but CTE, if you're tuning in solely to see Mr. Fallon, you'll need to set your VCR for tomorrow night because we're focusing on some of the other priorities this evening.

166
00:18:24,343 --> 00:18:28,264
On the personnel side, again, you've heard us, hopefully there's nothing here that

167
00:18:29,229 --> 00:18:31,051
is jumping out to anyone as a surprise.

168
00:18:31,111 --> 00:18:33,034
The reason that we update you on a quarterly basis.

169
00:18:33,074 --> 00:18:34,496
The reason why we solicit your feedback.

170
00:18:34,837 --> 00:18:42,528
The reason why we come before you to discuss these issues is so that both the members of the school committee and frankly for members of the community at large.

171
00:18:43,230 --> 00:18:44,611
there is a general awareness.

172
00:18:44,631 --> 00:19:07,389
We don't expect people to be able to detail every line item in the $67.4 million budget, but we hope that for people that are invested in the school system and paying attention to these conversations, there's a general understanding as to what it is that we're trying to do for the Medford Public Schools, and maybe as importantly, why it is we think that that will have an impact on student outcomes.

173
00:19:08,050 --> 00:19:11,493
Okay, so I think we have one more slide of our high-level priorities, and again,

174
00:19:12,951 --> 00:19:19,293
we'll be going through with some greater specificity tonight as to what are the priorities that we're connecting to each of these.

175
00:19:19,633 --> 00:19:27,655
And then, as I said, there'll be some that we'll talk a little bit more about tomorrow at the at the second budget meeting.

176
00:19:27,736 --> 00:19:35,418
So one of the changes that the school committee put in place the recommendation of the administration is that we're

177
00:19:36,191 --> 00:19:39,954
pursuing these meetings and having these discussions along thematic lines.

178
00:19:40,414 --> 00:19:51,682
And so, as the mayor said at the beginning of the meeting, the four themes that we had identified were, one, how are we investing to provide equitable supports, particularly for high need and most vulnerable student populations?

179
00:19:52,403 --> 00:19:58,947
Two, how are we recovering and continuing to mitigate the impact of the disruptions caused by the pandemic?

180
00:20:00,028 --> 00:20:05,472
Three, what are the technology investments that we're seeking to make, understanding the critical role

181
00:20:06,017 --> 00:20:08,178
that technology plays in our students instruction.

182
00:20:08,738 --> 00:20:19,242
And number four, how we structured as an organization, and how are we seeing some of the how some of these priorities and changes are affecting how we're organized and how we're pursuing that work.

183
00:20:19,562 --> 00:20:27,486
So tonight primarily will be on those first two objectives, talking about the bounce back from the pandemic and the supports to high needs students.

184
00:20:28,086 --> 00:20:28,206
And

185
00:20:29,208 --> 00:20:47,800
Again, the sort of peculiar thing about budgeting in FY23 for school districts really across the country, and certainly in Massachusetts, is identifying how it is that we use the significant injection of federal money that has become available to us, but that we know is very time limited.

186
00:20:48,280 --> 00:20:58,887
And so one of the points that you heard me make a lot during last year's budget presentation was that we know we don't want to commit one-time financial resources to recurring expenses.

187
00:20:59,787 --> 00:21:14,778
However, we also know that some of those, some of our financial priorities may well be, or challenges may well be recurring, and the need is exacerbated by the challenges that our students have faced over the course of the past couple of years.

188
00:21:15,138 --> 00:21:27,967
